/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/a-plus-core2.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("a-plus-core2", [["ap1",0,1,"A small business wants its new laptops joined to an Active Directory domain and encrypted with BitLocker. Which is the lowest Windows 11 edition that supports both?",["Home","Pro","Enterprise","Education"],1,"Windows 11 Pro supports domain join and BitLocker. Home supports neither; Enterprise and Education also do, but they are higher-tier volume editions.","Objective 1.3",["Windows 11 Home cannot join an Active Directory domain and lacks full BitLocker management (it only offers basic Device Encryption on some hardware).",null,"Enterprise supports both features, but it is a higher-tier volume-licensed edition, so it is not the lowest edition that qualifies.","Education supports both features, but it is an academic volume-licensed edition above Pro, so it is not the lowest that qualifies."],2],["ap2",0,1,"A technician must copy a 6 GB video to a USB flash drive that has to work on both Windows and macOS. Which file system should the drive use?",["FAT32","NTFS","ext4","exFAT"],3,"exFAT is read-write on both OSs and has no 4 GB file limit. FAT32 cannot hold files over 4 GB, and macOS mounts NTFS read-only by default.","Objective 1.1",["FAT32 works on both Windows and macOS but limits a single file to 4 GB, so a 6 GB video will not fit.","NTFS handles large files, but macOS mounts NTFS volumes read-only by default, so the Mac could not write to the drive.","ext4 is a Linux file system that neither Windows nor macOS can read or write natively.",null],1],["ap3",0,1,"A new 4 TB drive must be set up as one volume on a UEFI-based PC. Which partition style should be used?",["GPT","MBR","Extended","Dynamic spanned"],0,"GPT supports volumes far larger than 2 TB and is required for UEFI boot. MBR tops out at 2 TB.","Objective 1.2",[null,"MBR can only address disks up to 2 TB and is not used for native UEFI boot, so a 4 TB single volume would waste space.","An extended partition is an MBR workaround for creating more than four partitions; it does not lift MBR's 2 TB limit.","A dynamic spanned volume joins space from several disks into one volume; it is not a partition style and is unneeded for a single disk."],1],["ap4",0,1,"Which command repairs corrupted protected Windows system files by replacing them with cached copies?",["chkdsk /r","gpupdate /force","sfc /scannow","diskpart"],2,"System File Checker (sfc /scannow) verifies and replaces protected system files. chkdsk repairs file system errors and bad sectors, not system files.","Objective 1.5",["chkdsk /r checks the file system and locates bad sectors, but it does not verify or restore protected Windows system files.","gpupdate /force reapplies Group Policy settings; it has nothing to do with repairing system files.",null,"diskpart manages disks, partitions and volumes; it cannot check or replace corrupted system files."],1],["ap5",0,1,"sfc /scannow reports it found corrupt files but could not repair some of them. What should the technician run next?",["DISM /Online /Cleanup-Image /RestoreHealth","gpresult /r","format C: /q","netstat -an"],0,"DISM repairs the component store that sfc uses as its source; after it finishes, run sfc again.","Objective 1.5",[null,"gpresult /r only reports which Group Policy settings apply to the user and computer; it repairs nothing.","Formatting C: erases the whole operating system and user data, a drastic step when DISM can repair the component store.","netstat -an lists network connections and listening ports; it has no role in repairing Windows files."],3],["ap6",0,1,"An admin changed a Group Policy setting and wants a user's PC to apply it immediately rather than waiting. Which command should the user run?",["gpresult /r","gpupdate /force","gpresult /h report.html","whoami /groups"],1,"gpupdate /force reapplies all computer and user policies now. gpresult /r only reports which policies are applied.","Objective 1.5",["gpresult /r shows the resulting set of policy currently applied, but it does not trigger a new policy refresh.",null,"gpresult /h writes an HTML report of applied policies; it is a reporting tool and does not apply anything.","whoami /groups lists the security groups in the user's token; it does not refresh Group Policy."],1],["ap7",0,1,"A technician needs to copy a large folder tree to a file server, keep permissions and resume if the network drops. Which command fits best?",["copy","xcopy","diskpart","robocopy"],3,"Robocopy handles large trees, can copy NTFS permissions (/COPYALL) and retries or restarts in restartable mode. copy and xcopy lack these features.","Objective 1.5",["copy handles individual files, not whole folder trees, and cannot keep NTFS permissions or resume after a network drop.","xcopy can copy folder trees, but it lacks robocopy's restartable mode, automatic retries and full permission copying.","diskpart manages disk partitions and volumes; it does not copy files at all.",null],2],["ap8",0,1,"A user wants a mapped drive letter P: that points to \\\\fs01\\projects. Which command creates it?",["net user P: \\\\fs01\\projects","diskpart assign letter=P","nslookup P: fs01","net use P: \\\\fs01\\projects"],3,"net use maps a drive letter to a network share. net user manages user accounts, and diskpart assigns letters to local volumes.","Objective 1.5",["net user creates and manages local user accounts; it cannot map a drive letter to a share.","diskpart's assign command gives a drive letter to a local volume, not to a network share path.","nslookup queries DNS for name resolution; it does not create drive mappings.",null],1],["ap9",0,1,"A technician wants to see which programs and services run at every startup and schedule a job to run a script nightly. Which two tools should they use?",["Task Manager's Startup apps tab and Task Scheduler","Disk Management and Device Manager","Certificate Manager and Event Viewer","Registry Editor and Disk Cleanup"],0,"Task Manager shows startup impact and lets you disable startup apps; Task Scheduler runs programs on a schedule.","Objective 1.4",[null,"Disk Management handles volumes and Device Manager handles hardware drivers; neither manages startup apps or scheduled jobs.","Certificate Manager manages certificates and Event Viewer shows logs; neither controls startup programs or scheduling.","Registry Editor can show Run keys but is risky and indirect, and Disk Cleanup only removes temporary files; neither schedules jobs."],2],["ap10",0,1,"A printer driver keeps causing errors. Which tool lets the technician roll back the driver to the previous version?",["Performance Monitor","Local Users and Groups","Device Manager","System Information"],2,"Device Manager's driver tab offers Roll Back Driver. System Information only reports hardware and driver details.","Objective 1.4",["Performance Monitor tracks performance counters over time; it cannot change or roll back drivers.","Local Users and Groups manages accounts and group memberships, not device drivers.",null,"System Information reports hardware and driver details read-only; it has no option to roll back a driver."],1],["ap11",0,1,"A technician needs a full report of the PC's hardware, BIOS version and loaded drivers without changing anything. Which tool is best?",["msconfig","diskmgmt.msc","regedit","msinfo32"],3,"System Information (msinfo32) is a read-only report of hardware resources, components and software environment.","Objective 1.4",["msconfig changes boot and startup options; it is a configuration tool, not a full hardware and driver report.","Disk Management shows and changes disks and volumes only; it does not report BIOS version or drivers.","Registry Editor edits the registry directly, which risks changes and is not a readable inventory report.",null],2],["ap12",0,1,"A laptop is set to a Public network profile at a coffee shop. What does this change compared with a Private profile?",["The PC receives a static IP address","DHCP is disabled for the adapter","The VPN client connects automatically on every sign-in","Network discovery and file sharing are turned off"],3,"The Public profile hides the PC from others by disabling discovery and sharing. It does not change how the address is assigned.","Objective 1.7",["Network profiles don't control addressing; the PC keeps using whatever DHCP or static setting it already had.","The Public profile does not disable DHCP; it only changes firewall rules, discovery and sharing.","Auto-connecting a VPN is a separate VPN client setting, not something the Public profile turns on.",null],3],["ap13",0,1,"A user on a limited cellular hotspot complains that Windows Update used most of their data allowance. Which setting should be enabled?",["Metered connection","Hibernate after 15 minutes idle","Fast startup","Hibernate"],0,"Marking a connection as metered makes Windows defer most update downloads and background data use.","Objective 1.7",[null,"Hibernating after idle time only saves battery power; it does not stop Windows Update from downloading data.","Fast startup shortens boot time by partly hibernating the kernel; it has no effect on update data use.","Hibernate is a power state that saves RAM to disk; it doesn't limit background downloads while the PC is on."],1],["ap14",0,1,"A Windows PC has an IP of 192.168.10.25 but cannot reach any website by name, although pinging 8.8.8.8 works. Which setting is most likely wrong?",["Subnet mask","Default gateway","DNS server","Proxy exception list"],2,"Reaching an internet IP proves routing and the gateway work; failing only by name points to DNS.","Objective 1.7",["A wrong subnet mask would usually break communication with the gateway or other hosts, yet the PC reaches 8.8.8.8 fine.","Pinging an internet IP succeeds, which proves the default gateway is working.",null,"A proxy exception list only affects which sites bypass a proxy; it would not break name resolution for every site."],3],["ap15",0,1,"A Mac user needs automatic hourly backups of their files to an external drive. Which built-in feature should the technician set up?",["FileVault","Time Machine","Keychain Access","Spotlight"],1,"Time Machine backs up to an external or network drive automatically. FileVault is full-disk encryption.","Objective 1.8",["FileVault is macOS full-disk encryption; it protects data at rest but does not make backups.",null,"Keychain Access stores passwords, keys and certificates; it doesn't back up user files.","Spotlight is the macOS search feature; it indexes files but does not copy them anywhere."],1],["ap16",0,1,"An app on a Mac has frozen and will not respond to its own Quit command. What is the quickest built-in way to close it?",["Open Disk Utility and run First Aid","Open Mission Control and swipe up","Delete the app from the Applications folder","Press Command+Option+Esc and use Force Quit"],3,"Force Quit Applications ends a hung app. Mission Control only arranges windows, and deleting the app does not stop a running process.","Objective 1.8",["Disk Utility First Aid checks and repairs disk and file-system errors; it doesn't end a frozen app.","Mission Control only shows and arranges open windows and desktops; it doesn't terminate a hung process.","Deleting the app bundle removes it from disk, but the running process stays in memory and remains frozen.",null],1],["ap17",0,1,"On a Linux server, a script file must be executable by its owner only. Which command sets this?",["sudo 700 script.sh","chown 700 script.sh","chmod 777 script.sh","chmod 700 script.sh"],3,"chmod 700 gives the owner read, write and execute and removes all rights from group and others. chown changes ownership, not permissions.","Objective 1.9",["sudo runs a command with root privileges; '700' is not a command, so this does nothing to the file's permissions.","chown changes a file's owner and group, not its permission bits, so it can't make the file owner-executable.","chmod 777 gives read, write and execute to owner, group and everyone else, the opposite of owner-only access.",null],2],["ap18",0,1,"A technician must install a package on an Ubuntu workstation. Which command should they use?",["sudo dnf install htop","sudo apt install htop","chmod +x htop","ps htop"],1,"Ubuntu and Debian use the apt package manager, and installs need root, so sudo apt install. dnf is used on Red Hat-based distributions.","Objective 1.9",["dnf is the package manager for Red Hat-based distributions such as Fedora and RHEL, not Ubuntu.",null,"chmod +x only marks an existing file as executable; it does not download or install a package.","ps lists running processes; it cannot install software."],1],["ap19",0,1,"Which Linux file stores the hashed user passwords?",["/etc/passwd","/etc/hosts","/etc/shadow","/etc/fstab"],2,"/etc/shadow holds password hashes and is readable only by root. /etc/passwd lists accounts but no longer stores the hashes.","Objective 1.9",["/etc/passwd lists user accounts and is world-readable; on modern Linux the hashes were moved out of it into /etc/shadow.","/etc/hosts maps hostnames to IP addresses for local name resolution; it holds no passwords.",null,"/etc/fstab defines which file systems mount at boot and where; it has nothing to do with passwords."],1],["ap20",0,1,"A user wants to install a 3D design program on a laptop running 32-bit Windows 10 with 4 GB RAM. The vendor requires a 64-bit OS and 16 GB RAM. What is the best advice?",["Install it anyway, then enlarge the page file to make up for the missing RAM","Run it in compatibility mode","Install it from an ISO instead of a download","The laptop does not meet requirements; upgrade the hardware and OS or use another PC"],3,"A 64-bit application cannot run on a 32-bit OS, and RAM below the minimum cannot be fixed by settings. The system must meet requirements.","Objective 1.10",["A bigger page file cannot replace missing physical RAM, and it can't make a 64-bit program run on a 32-bit OS.","Compatibility mode imitates older Windows versions for old apps; it cannot run 64-bit code on a 32-bit OS or add RAM.","The installation media makes no difference; the program still needs a 64-bit OS and 16 GB of RAM.",null],3],["ap21",0,1,"IT needs to install the same standard Windows build on 60 new desktops that support network boot. Which method is most efficient?",["Image deployment over PXE","A clean install from a DVD on each PC","An in-place upgrade on each PC","A repair installation on each PC"],0,"PXE boot with image deployment sends one prepared image to many PCs over the network, instead of installing each one by hand.","Objective 1.2",[null,"Installing from DVD on each of 60 PCs is slow manual work and leads to inconsistent builds.","An in-place upgrade keeps an existing OS and its settings; new desktops need a clean standard build, and doing each by hand is slow.","A repair installation fixes a broken existing Windows install; it is not a way to deploy a standard build to new machines."],2],["ap22",0,1,"A Windows 10 PC is being upgraded to Windows 11, but the upgrade check fails. Which missing hardware feature is the most likely cause on an older PC?",["An NVMe drive","TPM 2.0","A dedicated GPU","A Wi-Fi 6 adapter"],1,"Windows 11 requires TPM 2.0 and UEFI with Secure Boot capability. NVMe, a dedicated GPU and Wi-Fi 6 are not requirements.","Objective 1.2",["Windows 11 runs on SATA or NVMe drives; it only needs 64 GB of storage, not a specific drive interface.",null,"Windows 11 needs a DirectX 12-compatible graphics adapter, which integrated graphics meet; a dedicated GPU isn't required.","Wi-Fi 6 is not a Windows 11 requirement; the OS runs with older Wi-Fi adapters or a wired connection."],2],["ap23",0,1,"A new employee needs email, shared documents and video meetings from any device, with files synced to their laptop. What should the technician set up first?",["A local mail client with POP3 only","A mapped drive to the on-premises file server plus a local copy of Office","A user account and license in the company's cloud productivity suite","A VPN to a home router"],2,"Cloud productivity tools start with an account and license assignment, then sync and app setup on each device.","Objective 1.11",["POP3 downloads mail to one device and doesn't sync across devices, and it provides no shared documents or meetings.","A mapped drive only works on the office network or VPN, and it doesn't provide cloud sync or meetings from any device.",null,"A VPN to a home router gives network access to that home network; it provides no email, documents or meetings."],2],["ap24",0,1,"Which Windows power setting saves the contents of RAM to disk and powers off completely, using no battery while off?",["Sleep","Screen saver","Fast startup","Hibernate"],3,"Hibernate writes memory to hiberfil.sys and turns the PC off. Sleep keeps RAM powered, so it still uses some battery.","Objective 1.6",["Sleep keeps RAM powered so the PC can resume quickly, so it still draws some battery.","A screen saver only covers or blanks the display while the PC keeps running at full power.","Fast startup saves the kernel session on shutdown to speed the next boot; it doesn't keep your open apps and documents.",null],1],["ap25",0,1,"A technician must extend a data volume into unallocated space next to it on the same disk using a graphical tool. Which tool should they open?",["Disk Cleanup","Resource Monitor","Defragment and Optimize Drives","Disk Management"],3,"Disk Management (diskmgmt.msc) creates, formats, extends and shrinks volumes. Disk Cleanup only deletes temporary files.","Objective 1.4",["Disk Cleanup frees space by deleting temporary and unneeded files; it cannot resize or extend volumes.","Resource Monitor shows live CPU, memory, disk and network use; it does not manage volumes.","Defragment and Optimize Drives reorganizes data or TRIMs SSDs; it cannot change a volume's size.",null],2],["ap26",0,2,"A user's PC shows ransom notes and many files have new extensions. According to CompTIA's malware removal procedure, what should the technician do right after verifying the symptoms?",["Educate the user","Disable System Restore","Quarantine the infected system","Run a full scan with updated definitions"],2,"The order is investigate/verify, quarantine, disable System Restore, remediate, schedule scans and updates, re-enable System Restore, educate. Isolating the PC stops the spread.","Objective 2.6",["Educating the user is the final step of the procedure, after the malware is removed.","Disabling System Restore comes after the system has been quarantined, so the malware can't spread in the meantime.",null,"Remediation with updated tools comes after quarantine and disabling System Restore, not right after verifying symptoms."],2],["ap27",0,2,"During malware removal, why does the technician disable System Restore before cleaning a Windows PC?",["It prevents malware in restore points from being brought back later","It speeds up the anti-malware scan","Windows won't allow an anti-malware scan while System Restore is running","It removes the malware from memory"],0,"Restore points can hold infected files; deleting them by disabling System Restore stops reinfection from a restore.","Objective 2.6",[null,"Disabling System Restore is not a performance tweak; the goal is removing infected restore points.","Windows lets anti-malware scans run while System Restore is enabled; the concern is reinfection from restore points.","Disabling System Restore deletes restore points on disk; it does nothing to malware running in memory."],3],["ap28",0,2,"The malware has been removed and scans are scheduled. What is the final step of the procedure?",["Re-image the computer","Quarantine the PC again","Disable the user's account","Educate the end user"],3,"After re-enabling System Restore and creating a clean restore point, the last step is teaching the user how to avoid reinfection.","Objective 2.6",["Re-imaging is a remediation option when cleaning fails; it is not the closing step of the procedure.","Quarantine happens early in the procedure; isolating the cleaned PC again at the end would make no sense.","Disabling the account punishes the user without fixing the cause; the procedure ends by educating them.",null],2],["ap29",0,2,"A shared folder grants Everyone Full Control at the share level, while NTFS gives the Sales group Read. What access does a Sales user have over the network?",["Full Control","No access","Modify","Read"],3,"When share and NTFS permissions combine, the more restrictive applies, so the user gets Read.","Objective 2.2",["Full Control is only the share-level permission; the more restrictive NTFS Read permission limits the user.","The user has permissions at both levels, so access is not denied; the combined result is Read.","Neither the share nor the NTFS permissions grant Modify, so the user can't get it.",null],3],["ap30",0,2,"A file with NTFS permissions is moved to a different folder on the same NTFS volume. What happens to its permissions?",["They are replaced by the destination's permissions","They are reset to Everyone Full Control","The file keeps its original permissions","The file becomes read-only"],2,"A move within the same volume keeps the file's permissions. A copy, or a move to another volume, takes the destination's inherited permissions.","Objective 2.2",["Inheriting the destination's permissions happens on a copy or a move to a different volume, not a move within the same volume.","Windows never resets permissions to Everyone Full Control when files are moved.",null,"Moving a file doesn't set its read-only attribute; its NTFS permissions stay as they were."],3],["ap31",0,2,"A company wants USB flash drives encrypted with a password so data is protected if a drive is lost. Which Windows feature fits?",["EFS","Windows Hello","User Account Control","BitLocker To Go"],3,"BitLocker To Go encrypts removable drives. EFS encrypts individual files for a user on NTFS volumes, and UAC controls elevation prompts.","Objective 2.2",["EFS encrypts individual files for one user on NTFS volumes; it isn't designed for password-protecting whole removable drives.","Windows Hello is biometric or PIN sign-in to Windows; it doesn't encrypt USB drives.","User Account Control prompts before elevating privileges; it provides no encryption at all.",null],1],["ap32",0,2,"Users often run as local administrators, and malware silently installs software. Which control best applies least privilege?",["Give users standard accounts and keep UAC enabled","Turn off Windows Defender Firewall","Enable the Guest account","Add users to the local Power Users group instead of Administrators"],0,"Standard accounts with UAC prompts for elevation stop software from installing silently with admin rights.","Objective 2.2",[null,"Turning off the firewall weakens security and does nothing to stop software from installing with admin rights.","The Guest account adds an unauthenticated way to sign in, which increases risk instead of applying least privilege.","Power Users is a legacy group that still has elevated rights; it is not a least-privilege account type."],2],["ap33",0,2,"An office replacing its Wi-Fi wants each employee to sign in with their own directory credentials instead of a shared passphrase. What should it use?",["WPA2-Personal with TKIP","WPA3-Enterprise with RADIUS","WPA3-Personal with SAE","An open network with MAC filtering"],1,"Enterprise mode uses 802.1X with a RADIUS server so each user authenticates individually. Personal modes use one shared passphrase.","Objective 2.3",["WPA2-Personal uses one shared passphrase for everyone, and TKIP is a deprecated, weak cipher.",null,"WPA3-Personal with SAE is stronger than WPA2-Personal but still uses one shared passphrase, not per-user credentials.","An open network has no encryption, and MAC filtering is easily bypassed by spoofing; it gives no per-user sign-in."],2],["ap34",0,2,"Which authentication protocol uses tickets issued by a key distribution center and is used by Active Directory domain logons?",["RADIUS","TACACS+","Kerberos","Diameter"],2,"Kerberos issues time-limited tickets from the domain controller's KDC. RADIUS and TACACS+ are AAA protocols for network access and device administration.","Objective 2.3",["RADIUS is an AAA protocol for network access such as Wi-Fi and VPN; it doesn't use KDC tickets.","TACACS+ is an AAA protocol for administering network devices; it doesn't issue tickets for domain logons.",null,"Diameter is a successor to RADIUS used mainly in telecom networks; it isn't used for Active Directory logons."],1],["ap35",0,2,"Network admins need an AAA protocol for logging in to switches and routers that encrypts the whole payload and separates authorization from authentication. Which protocol fits?",["LDAP","RADIUS","Kerberos","TACACS+"],3,"TACACS+ encrypts the full packet and handles authentication, authorization and accounting separately, which suits device administration. RADIUS encrypts only the password.","Objective 2.3",["LDAP is a directory query protocol; it is not an AAA protocol and doesn't separate authorization from authentication.","RADIUS encrypts only the password and combines authentication with authorization, so it doesn't fit the requirements.","Kerberos handles authentication with tickets for domain resources; it isn't the AAA protocol used for switch and router logins.",null],3],["ap36",0,2,"A user's PC shows no symptoms, but a tool detects that a hidden kernel-level program is concealing processes from Task Manager. What type of malware is this?",["Adware","Keylogger","Rootkit","Cryptominer"],2,"Rootkits hide at a low level, often the kernel or boot process, and conceal their files and processes from normal tools.","Objective 2.4",["Adware displays unwanted ads and is usually very visible; it doesn't typically hide at kernel level.","A keylogger records keystrokes; hiding processes at the kernel level is the defining trait of a different type.",null,"A cryptominer uses CPU or GPU heavily, which would cause obvious symptoms; concealing processes defines a rootkit."],1],["ap37",0,2,"A PC's CPU runs near 100% even when idle, the fan is loud and there is no obvious app open. Which malware is most likely?",["Spyware","Ransomware","Boot sector virus","Cryptominer"],3,"Cryptominers use CPU or GPU cycles to mine cryptocurrency, causing constant high load. Ransomware would encrypt files and demand payment.","Objective 2.4",["Spyware quietly collects information and usually doesn't cause constant near-100% CPU load.","Ransomware encrypts files and shows a ransom demand, which is not the symptom described.","A boot sector virus infects startup code and causes boot problems, not constant high CPU use while idle.",null],2],["ap38",0,2,"An employee gets a text message saying their payroll account is locked, with a link to sign in. What attack is this?",["Vishing","Whaling","Smishing","Tailgating"],2,"Smishing is phishing by SMS. Vishing uses voice calls, and whaling targets senior executives.","Objective 2.5",["Vishing is phishing by voice call, but this attack arrived as a text message.","Whaling targets senior executives, usually by email; this is a generic text sent to an employee.",null,"Tailgating is following an authorized person through a secure door; it is a physical attack, not a message."],1],["ap39",0,2,"A poster in the break room has a QR code offering a free lunch coupon, which leads to a fake company sign-in page. What is this?",["Evil twin","QR code phishing","Dumpster diving","Shoulder surfing"],1,"QR code phishing hides a malicious URL in a QR code so users can't easily inspect the link before scanning.","Objective 2.5",["An evil twin is a rogue Wi-Fi access point copying a real SSID; no QR code is involved.",null,"Dumpster diving is searching trash for useful information; it doesn't involve luring users to a fake page.","Shoulder surfing is watching someone's screen or keyboard to steal information, not planting a malicious link."],1],["ap40",0,2,"An attacker sets up a hotspot in the lobby with the same name as the company guest Wi-Fi to capture traffic. What is this?",["Zero-day exploit","Brute-force attack","SQL injection","Evil twin"],3,"An evil twin is a rogue access point that copies a legitimate SSID so users connect to it.","Objective 2.5",["A zero-day exploit attacks an unknown, unpatched software flaw; it doesn't involve a fake hotspot.","A brute-force attack guesses passwords or keys repeatedly; it doesn't imitate a Wi-Fi network.","SQL injection sends malicious input to a database-backed application, not a rogue wireless network.",null],1],["ap41",0,2,"The accounts payable team receives an email that appears to come from a regular supplier's real mailbox, asking them to change the bank account for invoice payments. What attack is this?",["Distributed denial of service","Cross-site scripting","Business email compromise","Impersonation by telephone"],2,"BEC uses a compromised or spoofed business mailbox to redirect payments. Verify any bank change by calling a known number.","Objective 2.5",["A DDoS floods a service with traffic to take it offline; it doesn't trick staff into redirecting payments.","Cross-site scripting injects malicious scripts into web pages viewed by others; it's not an email payment fraud.",null,"Impersonation by telephone is vishing; this attack came through the supplier's email mailbox, not a call."],2],["ap42",0,2,"Old hard disk drives with customer data must be destroyed so that a reuse vendor can't recover anything, and the company needs proof. What is the best approach?",["Run a full format on every drive and then donate them all to a local school","Delete the partitions with diskpart","Move the files to the Recycle Bin and empty it","Have a certified vendor shred them and issue a certificate of destruction"],3,"Physical shredding makes the data unrecoverable, and a certificate of destruction documents it. Formatting and deleting leave recoverable data.","Objective 2.8",["A full format can leave data recoverable with forensic tools, and donating the drives provides no proof of destruction.","Deleting partitions only removes the partition table; the data stays on the platters and is easily recovered.","Emptying the Recycle Bin only marks the space as free; the files remain recoverable, and there's no proof.",null],3],["ap43",0,2,"A technician plans to degauss a batch of retired SSDs. Why is this a poor choice?",["SSDs store data in flash memory, not magnetically","Degaussing voids the warranty","Degaussing only works on encrypted drives","SSDs must be formatted and partitioned before they can be degaussed"],0,"Degaussing destroys magnetic media such as HDDs and tapes. SSDs need secure erase, crypto-erase or physical destruction.","Objective 2.8",[null,"The warranty on retired drives is irrelevant; the real issue is that degaussing doesn't affect flash storage.","Degaussing works on magnetic media whether or not it is encrypted; encryption has nothing to do with it.","Formatting or partitioning doesn't make an SSD vulnerable to degaussing; flash memory isn't magnetic."],3],["ap44",0,2,"Which change to a new SOHO router is most important to do first?",["Enable UPnP for gaming consoles","Hide the SSID","Change the default administrator password","Turn on WPS so new devices can pair with one button press"],2,"Default admin credentials are publicly known. UPnP and WPS weaken security, and hiding the SSID adds little protection.","Objective 2.9",["UPnP lets devices open router ports automatically, which weakens security instead of improving it.","Hiding the SSID adds little protection, because the network name can still be found with simple wireless tools.",null,"WPS push-button and PIN pairing have known weaknesses, so enabling it lowers security."],2],["ap45",0,2,"A home user wants a security camera app reachable from the internet on TCP 8443 through the router. What must be configured?",["DHCP reservation only","Content filtering for the camera's web traffic","Port forwarding to the camera's IP","A guest network"],2,"Port forwarding sends inbound traffic on a port to an internal host; pair it with a DHCP reservation so the address doesn't change.","Objective 2.9",["A DHCP reservation only keeps the camera's IP address the same; it doesn't let inbound internet traffic reach it.","Content filtering blocks or allows outbound web content; it doesn't open an inbound port to the camera.",null,"A guest network isolates visitors' devices; it doesn't make an internal device reachable from the internet."],2],["ap46",0,2,"A user downloaded an installer from a mirror site. How can they confirm the file was not altered?",["Check that the file name and icon match the product name","Compare its hash with the one published by the vendor","Check that the download used HTTP","Right-click it and choose Run as administrator"],1,"A matching SHA-256 hash proves the file is identical to the one the vendor published.","Objective 2.10",["An attacker can easily keep the same file name and icon on a modified installer, so they prove nothing.",null,"HTTP is unencrypted and doesn't verify integrity, so it offers no proof the file wasn't changed.","Running as administrator gives the file more power over the system; it verifies nothing and raises risk."],1],["ap47",0,2,"A company wants lost corporate phones to be locatable and erasable, and wants to enforce screen locks and encryption on every device. What should it deploy?",["A VPN client on every phone","A password manager","Mobile device management","Bluetooth pairing"],2,"MDM enforces policies such as passcodes and encryption and supports locate and remote wipe.","Objective 2.7",["A VPN client encrypts network traffic but cannot locate, wipe or enforce screen locks on a phone.","A password manager stores credentials securely; it can't enforce device policies or remotely wipe phones.",null,"Bluetooth pairing connects accessories to a phone; it has no device-management capability."],2],["ap48",0,2,"Which workstation hardening step stops malware on an inserted USB drive from launching automatically?",["Enable remote desktop","Enable the Guest account","Turn off screen saver locks","Disable AutoRun/AutoPlay"],3,"Disabling AutoRun and AutoPlay stops media from executing content when it is inserted.","Objective 2.7",["Enabling Remote Desktop opens a remote access path, which adds attack surface and does nothing about USB media.","The Guest account allows unauthenticated sign-ins, which weakens security and doesn't affect USB autorun.","Turning off screen saver locks leaves unattended PCs open to anyone; it doesn't stop USB malware from launching.",null],2],["ap49",0,2,"Employees must badge in through a door, wait in a small room until that door closes, and then badge again through a second door. What is this control?",["Bollard","Access control vestibule","Badge reader with motion sensor","Video surveillance"],1,"An access control vestibule (mantrap) lets only one door open at a time, which stops tailgating.","Objective 2.1",["Bollards are short, sturdy posts that stop vehicles from reaching a building; they don't control people at doors.",null,"A badge reader with a motion sensor controls one door; it doesn't create the two-door, one-at-a-time space described.","Video surveillance records activity but doesn't physically stop anyone from entering."],1],["ap50",0,2,"A company wants to block employees from emailing files that contain credit card numbers outside the organization. Which control fits?",["Data loss prevention","Single sign-on","Directory services with group policy","Screen lock policy"],0,"DLP inspects outgoing data for sensitive patterns such as card numbers and blocks or flags it.","Objective 2.1",[null,"Single sign-on lets users sign in once to reach many apps; it doesn't inspect outgoing data.","Directory services and Group Policy manage accounts and settings; they don't scan email content for card numbers.","A screen lock policy protects unattended PCs; it doesn't stop sensitive data from being emailed."],1],["ap51",0,3,"After a graphics driver update, a Windows PC shows a blue screen during every normal boot. What should the technician do first?",["Replace the video card","Boot into Safe Mode and roll back the driver","Reinstall Windows from scratch","Run chkdsk /r from the desktop after the next reboot"],1,"Safe Mode loads basic drivers, so the faulty driver can be rolled back. Reinstalling or replacing hardware is premature.","Objective 3.1",["Replacing the video card is premature; the crashes started after a driver update, which points to software.",null,"Reinstalling Windows is a drastic step that loses time and settings when rolling back one driver should fix it.","The PC can't reach the desktop because it crashes on every boot, and chkdsk fixes disk errors, not driver faults."],2],["ap52",0,3,"A PC started crashing after this week's Windows cumulative update. The user needs it working today. What is the best fix?",["Uninstall the latest quality update from Windows Update or WinRE","Disable Windows Update permanently","Reimage the PC with no updates","Replace the RAM"],0,"Rolling back the problem update fixes the regression quickly. Disabling updates permanently leaves the PC exposed.","Objective 3.1",[null,"Permanently disabling Windows Update leaves the PC without security fixes; it doesn't fix the current crash either.","Reimaging takes far longer than removing one update, and a PC with no updates is left exposed.","The crashes started with a software update, so replacing the RAM wouldn't address the likely cause."],3],["ap53",0,3,"A service required by an accounting app fails to start after reboots, and Event Viewer shows a dependency failed. What should the technician check?",["The screen resolution","The print spooler queue and the default printer setting","The services that it depends on in services.msc","The user's wallpaper settings"],2,"A service won't start if a service it depends on is stopped or disabled; the Dependencies tab lists them.","Objective 3.1",["Screen resolution affects display output only; it has no effect on whether a Windows service can start.","The print spooler and the default printer affect printing, not a failed dependency for an accounting service.",null,"Wallpaper settings are a cosmetic user preference and cannot cause a service dependency failure."],3],["ap54",0,3,"A user's PC is very slow. Task Manager shows memory at 98% and heavy disk activity while several large apps are open. What is the best long-term fix?",["Run Disk Cleanup","Add more RAM","Defragment the SSD","Disable the page file"],1,"Constant high memory use forces paging to disk, which causes the slowness. More RAM fixes the root cause; disabling the page file would cause crashes.","Objective 3.1",["Disk Cleanup frees storage space, but the problem is memory pressure, not a full disk.",null,"SSDs shouldn't be defragmented (Windows TRIMs them), and the slowness comes from paging due to low RAM.","Disabling the page file removes the overflow space for memory, which would cause apps to crash when RAM runs out."],2],["ap55",0,3,"A PC displays \"Operating system not found\" after a technician added a second drive. Nothing else changed. What is the most likely cause?",["The boot order now lists the new drive first","The monitor cable is loose","The Windows license expired when the hardware changed","The DNS server is down"],0,"Adding a drive can change the firmware boot order; set the OS drive first in UEFI/BIOS.","Objective 3.1",[null,"A loose monitor cable would cause a blank or flickering screen, not a firmware 'Operating system not found' message.","Windows licensing doesn't stop the boot loader from being found; a license problem shows up after Windows loads.","DNS is used for network name resolution; the PC hasn't loaded an OS yet, so network services aren't involved."],3],["ap56",0,3,"A user's clock keeps drifting several minutes, and they can't log on to the domain until it is corrected. What should the technician check?",["The display refresh rate","Whether the Windows Time service syncs to a valid NTP source","The size of the page file","The browser's pop-up blocker"],1,"Kerberos rejects logons when clocks differ too much. Windows Time must sync (w32tm /resync), and a failing CMOS battery can also cause drift.","Objective 3.1",["The display refresh rate only affects how often the screen redraws; it has nothing to do with the system clock.",null,"The page file is virtual memory; its size doesn't affect clock accuracy or domain logons.","The browser's pop-up blocker affects web pages only; it has no role in time sync or Kerberos logons."],3],["ap57",0,3,"Only one user on a shared PC gets a temporary profile at every sign-in and loses their desktop settings. Other users are fine. What is the best fix?",["Reinstall Windows","Replace the hard drive","Rebuild the user's profile","Reset the BIOS to defaults"],2,"A corrupt profile causes temporary profiles for that user only. Back up the data, then rebuild the profile.","Objective 3.1",["Other users on the same PC work fine, so the operating system is healthy and reinstalling it is excessive.","A failing hard drive would affect all users, not just one profile.",null,"BIOS settings apply to the whole machine, so resetting them can't fix one user's corrupt profile."],3],["ap58",0,3,"Windows warns that a USB controller has run out of resources when a user connects a dock, webcam and two drives to one hub. What should they try?",["Update the BIOS password","Spread devices across USB ports on different controllers","Disable Windows Firewall","Change the power plan to Balanced"],1,"Each USB controller has limited endpoints; moving devices to ports on other controllers (or removing the hub) clears the warning.","Objective 3.1",["A BIOS password controls access to firmware settings; it has nothing to do with USB controller resources.",null,"Windows Firewall filters network traffic; it has no effect on USB controller endpoints.","Power plans change performance and sleep behavior; they don't give a USB controller more endpoints."],3],["ap59",0,3,"A phone app closes immediately every time it is opened. Restarting the phone did not help. What should the technician try next?",["Clear the app's cache and data or reinstall it","Factory reset the phone","Replace the battery","Enable airplane mode"],0,"Clearing the app's cache or reinstalling it fixes corrupt app data without the data loss of a factory reset.","Objective 3.2",[null,"A factory reset erases all data on the phone, which is excessive when only one app is failing.","A worn battery affects battery life and shutdowns, not one app closing immediately on launch.","Airplane mode turns off wireless radios; it won't fix corrupt app data that makes the app crash."],2],["ap60",0,3,"A user's phone battery now lasts half a day. Battery settings show one social app used most of the power in the background. What is the best fix?",["Replace the phone","Restrict the app's background activity or update it","Turn off Wi-Fi permanently","Lower the screen brightness to zero and turn on dark mode"],1,"Battery usage shows the culprit; limiting background refresh or updating the misbehaving app addresses it directly.","Objective 3.2",["Replacing the phone is costly and unnecessary when one app's background activity is clearly the cause.",null,"Turning off Wi-Fi often increases battery drain as the phone uses cellular data, and it ignores the app causing it.","Minimum brightness and dark mode lower screen power, but the problem is one app's background use, not the display."],2],["ap61",0,3,"A tablet's screen won't rotate when turned sideways in any app. What should be checked first?",["Whether rotation lock is enabled","The Bluetooth pairing list","The storage space available","The Wi-Fi band"],0,"Rotation lock in the quick settings keeps the screen in one orientation; it is the most common cause.","Objective 3.2",[null,"The Bluetooth pairing list only controls wireless accessory connections; it doesn't affect screen orientation.","Available storage doesn't affect whether the display rotates with the device.","The Wi-Fi band changes network connection speed and range; it has no effect on screen rotation."],1],["ap62",0,3,"A user installed an app from a website link instead of the official store. Now the phone shows fake virus warnings and uses far more data. What is the most likely cause?",["A failing battery","A sideloaded malicious app","A weak Wi-Fi signal forcing cellular data","An expired SIM card"],1,"Apps sideloaded from outside the official store skip vetting and often carry malware that causes fake alerts and extra traffic.","Objective 3.3",["A failing battery causes short battery life or shutdowns, not fake virus warnings and extra data use.",null,"A weak Wi-Fi signal can increase cellular data use, but it wouldn't cause fake virus pop-ups.","An expired or faulty SIM stops cellular service; it doesn't create fake alerts or extra traffic."],2],["ap63",0,3,"An employee's phone was rooted to install custom themes. Why is this a security concern for the company?",["Rooting speeds up the processor","Rooting blocks all app updates","Rooting bypasses OS security controls and can break MDM enforcement","Rooting disables Bluetooth"],2,"Rooting or jailbreaking removes built-in sandboxing and restrictions, letting apps gain full control and letting users avoid MDM policy.","Objective 3.3",["Rooting doesn't speed up the processor; any tuning it allows is a side effect, not a security concern.","Rooted phones can still receive app updates; the concern is the loss of OS security controls.",null,"Rooting doesn't disable Bluetooth; the security concern is bypassing OS protections and MDM."],3],["ap64",0,3,"A user's browser shows a certificate warning on their bank's site, but only on the office network. Other sites are fine. What should the technician suspect?",["The browser cache is full","The site is being intercepted or the proxy is misconfigured","The browser's saved passwords for the bank have expired and need resetting","The PC needs more RAM"],1,"A certificate mismatch on one network can mean an on-path attack or a misconfigured inspecting proxy. Users should not click through it.","Objective 3.4",["A full browser cache can slow pages down but doesn't cause certificate warnings, especially on only one network.",null,"Saved passwords have nothing to do with certificate validation, and they don't expire in a way that causes warnings.","RAM has no effect on certificate validation; the warning depends on the network the PC is on."],3],["ap65",0,3,"Every search in a user's browser goes to an unfamiliar search site, and a new toolbar appeared. What should the technician do first?",["Reinstall the operating system","Replace the network card","Change the user's email password","Remove unknown extensions and reset the browser's search and home settings"],3,"Redirection and toolbars usually come from a malicious extension or hijacked settings. Remove them and then scan for malware.","Objective 3.4",["Reinstalling the OS is a drastic, time-consuming step when the problem is usually a browser extension or settings.","The network card can't change which search engine the browser uses or add toolbars.","Changing the email password is unrelated to browser redirection and doesn't remove the rogue extension.",null],2],["ap66",0,3,"A pop-up claims the PC is infected and tells the user to call a support number. The installed antivirus shows no alerts. What is this most likely?",["A real Windows Defender alert","A genuine warning from the PC's hardware diagnostics","A fake security alert (scareware)","A Windows Update notice"],2,"Fake antivirus alerts push users to call scammers or install malware. Close the browser and run a scan with the real antivirus.","Objective 3.4",["Real Windows Defender alerts appear in the Windows Security app and never ask users to call a phone number.","Hardware diagnostics report component faults; they don't show virus pop-ups with support numbers.",null,"Windows Update notices announce updates; they never claim infections or give a support number to call."],2],["ap67",0,3,"After a malware cleanup, a PC still can't reach any websites, though it has a valid IP. The technician finds a proxy set to 127.0.0.1:8080. What should they do?",["Remove the proxy setting and check the hosts file","Replace the Ethernet cable","Renew the DHCP lease only","Disable the firewall"],0,"Malware often sets a rogue proxy or hosts entries to hijack traffic. Clear them after removal.","Objective 3.4",[null,"A bad cable would break the link entirely; the PC has a valid IP, so the physical connection works.","Renewing the DHCP lease gives a fresh IP but leaves the rogue proxy in place, so web traffic still fails.","Disabling the firewall weakens security and doesn't remove the rogue proxy that is blocking web traffic."],3],["ap68",0,3,"Windows updates keep failing on a PC that recently had malware, and some system files appear renamed. What is the most likely explanation?",["The update server is offline for everyone","Malware altered system files and blocked updates","The PC is on a metered connection","The time zone is set incorrectly"],1,"Malware often tampers with system files and update services to avoid being removed. Run sfc and DISM, or reimage if it can't be trusted.","Objective 3.4",["An outage for everyone is unlikely and wouldn't explain renamed system files on this PC after malware.",null,"A metered connection only defers some update downloads; it doesn't make updates fail or rename system files.","An incorrect time zone doesn't make updates fail or rename system files (a badly wrong clock could break TLS, but not rename files)."],3],["ap69",0,3,"An app crashes several times a day. Where can the technician see a timeline of app failures and Windows changes to find what changed?",["Reliability Monitor","Disk Management","Device Manager","Local Security Policy"],0,"Reliability Monitor charts crashes, failures and installs by day, making it easy to connect a crash to a recent change.","Objective 3.1",[null,"Disk Management handles disks and volumes; it keeps no history of app crashes or system changes.","Device Manager shows hardware and drivers; it doesn't give a timeline of application failures.","Local Security Policy sets security settings such as password and audit policies; it doesn't track crashes."],2],["ap70",0,3,"A laptop shuts off without warning, mostly while gaming, and Event Viewer shows Kernel-Power errors. What should be checked first?",["The user's password age","Overheating: vents, fans and thermal readings","The screen brightness and display timeout settings","The Windows edition"],1,"Unexpected shutdowns under load often come from overheating protection. Clean vents and check fans and temperatures.","Objective 3.1",["Password age has nothing to do with the laptop powering off under load.",null,"Brightness and display timeout control when the screen dims or turns off; they don't cut power suddenly while gaming.","The Windows edition doesn't cause sudden shutdowns under load; heat or power problems do."],2],["ap71",0,3,"A user can't connect wireless earbuds to their phone, though they worked yesterday. Bluetooth is on. What is a good first step?",["Factory reset the phone","Remove (forget) the earbuds and pair them again","Disable Wi-Fi calling","Install an app from a third-party store"],1,"Forgetting the device and re-pairing clears a bad pairing record; it is quick and non-destructive.","Objective 3.2",["A factory reset wipes the phone, which is far too drastic for a pairing problem with one accessory.",null,"Wi-Fi calling routes calls over Wi-Fi; disabling it doesn't affect Bluetooth pairing.","Third-party stores add malware risk, and no extra app is needed to pair Bluetooth earbuds."],2],["ap72",0,4,"A technician is closing a ticket. What should the resolution notes include?",["Only the word \"fixed\"","The user's password","A clear description of the problem, steps taken and final fix","A personal opinion about how the user probably caused the problem"],2,"Good notes let others understand what happened and reuse the fix. Never record passwords in tickets.","Objective 4.1",["'Fixed' gives other technicians no information about the problem or solution, so the fix can't be reused.","Passwords must never be recorded in tickets, since anyone with ticket access could read them.",null,"Personal opinions blaming the user are unprofessional and don't help anyone resolve similar issues."],1],["ap73",0,4,"IT needs a central record of every laptop, who has it, its warranty date and its configuration. What should it use?",["An asset management database (CMDB) with asset tags","A shared desktop wallpaper","The DHCP lease table","A printed phone list"],0,"Asset management with tags and a CMDB tracks devices, assigned users, warranty and configuration.","Objective 4.1",[null,"Wallpaper can show a device name on one screen but can't serve as a central, searchable record of assets.","The DHCP lease table only shows current IP-to-MAC assignments; it has no owner, warranty or configuration data.","A printed phone list records contacts, not devices, and can't be searched or kept current centrally."],1],["ap74",0,4,"A new hire's first day requires an account, a laptop, email and building access. Which document keeps this consistent every time?",["An incident report","An acceptable use policy","A service-level agreement","An onboarding checklist"],3,"An onboarding (new user setup) checklist lists every step so nothing is missed. The AUP defines allowed use of systems.","Objective 4.1",["An incident report documents an event such as an outage or breach after it happens, not new-hire setup steps.","An acceptable use policy defines what users may do with company systems; it isn't a setup checklist.","An SLA defines service levels, such as response times, between a provider and a customer; it doesn't list setup steps.",null],1],["ap75",0,4,"Before a planned firewall change, the change advisory board asks what will happen if the change breaks production. What part of the request answers this?",["The rollback plan","The asset tag","The end-user acceptance","The purchase order"],0,"A rollback plan explains how to return to the previous working state if the change fails.","Objective 4.2",[null,"An asset tag identifies a piece of equipment; it doesn't describe how to undo a failed change.","End-user acceptance confirms the change works for users afterward; it doesn't explain what happens if it breaks production.","A purchase order covers buying goods or services; it has nothing to do with recovering from a failed change."],2],["ap76",0,4,"A backup job runs a full backup on Sunday and differential backups the other days. The server fails Thursday morning. What must be restored?",["Only Wednesday's differential","Sunday's full and every differential since","Sunday's full and Wednesday's differential","Only Sunday's full"],2,"A differential holds all changes since the last full, so you need the full plus the latest differential.","Objective 4.3",["A differential only holds changes since the last full backup, so it can't be restored without Sunday's full.","Each differential already includes all changes since the full, so earlier differentials aren't needed; that's the incremental method.",null,"Restoring only Sunday's full would lose every change made Monday through Wednesday."],3],["ap77",0,4,"A company wants to follow the 3-2-1 backup rule. Which setup meets it?",["Three copies of the data on two types of media, with one copy off-site","Three backups a day kept for two weeks, one per server","Three servers in two racks with one UPS","One full, two incremental and three differential backups"],0,"3-2-1 means three copies, two different media, one off-site.","Objective 4.3",[null,"Backup frequency and retention time are separate settings; they don't guarantee two media types or an off-site copy.","Server and rack counts describe hardware redundancy, not backup copies; a UPS is power protection, not a backup.","This describes a mix of backup types, not three copies on two media with one off-site."],1],["ap78",0,4,"Backups have run nightly for a year, but nobody knows if they work. What should the team do?",["Switch to incremental backups","Buy more backup storage","Perform regular test restores","Delete older backups"],2,"Only a restore test proves backups are usable and meet recovery time goals.","Objective 4.3",["Changing the backup type doesn't prove existing backups can be restored.","More storage keeps more backups but doesn't show that any of them actually work.",null,"Deleting older backups reduces recovery options and still doesn't verify the remaining ones."],2],["ap79",0,4,"Before replacing RAM in a desktop, what should a technician do to prevent electrostatic damage?",["Wear rubber gloves only","Leave the PC plugged in and powered on","Use a vacuum to clean the slots","Wear an ESD wrist strap connected to ground and use an ESD mat"],3,"An ESD strap and mat equalize charge so static doesn't discharge into components. Unplug the PC before working inside.","Objective 4.4",["Rubber gloves don't bleed static charge safely to ground, so they don't provide proper ESD protection.","Working inside a PC that is plugged in and on risks electric shock and damage to components.","A vacuum can build up static charge and damage components; compressed air is the usual choice.",null],1],["ap80",0,4,"A technician needs to know how to safely handle and dispose of a toner cartridge that leaked. Where should they look?",["The product's safety data sheet (SDS)","The printer's IP configuration","The service-level agreement with the printer vendor","The EULA"],0,"The SDS lists hazards, handling, spill clean-up and disposal instructions for a product.","Objective 4.5",[null,"The printer's IP configuration covers network settings; it has no information on handling hazardous toner.","An SLA defines vendor service levels and response times; it doesn't include material handling or disposal guidance.","The EULA covers software licensing terms; it doesn't describe how to handle chemical spills."],1],["ap81",0,4,"An office often has brief power flickers that reboot PCs and have corrupted a server's database. What should be installed?",["A surge suppressor only","A KVM switch","An uninterruptible power supply","A second monitor"],2,"A UPS provides battery power through short outages and allows a clean shutdown. A surge suppressor does not supply power.","Objective 4.5",["A surge suppressor only absorbs voltage spikes; it provides no power during flickers or outages.","A KVM switch lets one keyboard, monitor and mouse control several computers; it doesn't supply power.",null,"A second monitor adds screen space and has nothing to do with power problems."],2],["ap82",0,4,"While repairing a laptop, a technician sees files that appear to be illegal content. What should they do first?",["Delete the files and finish the repair","Stop, leave the evidence untouched and follow the incident process by notifying the proper authority","Copy the files to a USB drive for later","Tell coworkers about it"],1,"Preserve evidence, report through policy (management or law enforcement) and keep chain of custody. Deleting or copying damages the evidence.","Objective 4.6",["Deleting the files destroys evidence and may itself be illegal; the technician must preserve it.",null,"Copying the files to a USB drive changes the evidence, breaks chain of custody and may mean possessing illegal content.","Telling coworkers spreads confidential information and doesn't follow the proper reporting process."],3],["ap83",0,4,"A company has a license for 25 installs of an app and has installed it on 40 PCs. What does this violate?",["The software's license agreement (EULA)","The acceptable use policy of the ISP","GDPR","The service-level agreement with the help desk"],0,"The EULA sets how many installs or users are allowed; going beyond it is a licensing violation.","Objective 4.6",[null,"An ISP's acceptable use policy governs use of its internet service, not the install count of a software product.","GDPR regulates the handling of personal data of people in the EU; it doesn't deal with software install counts.","An SLA with the help desk sets support response times; it doesn't control how many copies of software may be installed."],1],["ap84",0,4,"A technician is on site to fix a slow PC and the customer is visibly angry about repeated problems. What is the best approach?",["Explain it's not your fault and move on","Argue about who caused the issue","Post about it on social media","Listen without interrupting, acknowledge the frustration and explain the next steps"],3,"Active listening, staying calm and setting clear expectations help settle a difficult customer.","Objective 4.7",["Brushing off the complaint makes the customer feel ignored and doesn't solve their problem.","Arguing about blame makes the situation worse and is unprofessional.","Posting about a customer on social media is unprofessional and may disclose confidential information.",null],2],["ap85",0,4,"A technician must remap the same network drives on 300 Windows PCs every time users sign in. Which script type fits best?",["A PowerShell (.ps1) script","A Bash (.sh) script","A macOS .dmg file","A Python notebook on the technician's phone"],0,"PowerShell is native to Windows and suits logon scripts that map drives. Bash scripts are for Linux and macOS shells.","Objective 4.8",[null,"Bash scripts run in Linux and macOS shells; Windows doesn't run them natively at sign-in.","A .dmg file is a macOS disk image for installing software; it isn't a script and doesn't run on Windows.","A notebook on the technician's phone can't run automatically on 300 Windows PCs as a logon script."],1],["ap86",0,4,"A script downloaded from a forum claims to speed up Windows. What is the main risk of running it on production PCs without review?",["It may be too short","It could introduce malware or change system settings unintentionally","It will switch every PC to the wrong keyboard layout and display language","It cannot run without Python"],1,"Untested scripts can carry malware, alter settings or crash systems. Review and test in a sandbox first.","Objective 4.8",["A script's length isn't a security risk; the danger is what the unreviewed code actually does.",null,"Changing the keyboard layout or display language is an unlikely, minor effect compared with malware or damaged settings.","Scripts can be written in many languages, such as PowerShell or batch; not needing Python isn't the concern."],2],["ap87",0,4,"A help desk needs to monitor, patch and remotely support hundreds of client PCs from one console. Which tool type fits?",["Remote monitoring and management (RMM)","VNC on each PC with a shared password","Telnet","Open RDP ports on each PC to the internet"],0,"RMM platforms centralize monitoring, patching, scripting and remote control. Exposing RDP or Telnet to the internet is unsafe.","Objective 4.9",[null,"VNC on each PC gives remote control only, with no central monitoring or patching, and a shared password is insecure.","Telnet is an unencrypted remote terminal protocol; it doesn't monitor or patch PCs.","Exposing RDP to the internet invites brute-force attacks and still gives no central monitoring or patching."],1],["ap88",0,4,"An admin needs secure command-line access to a Linux server across the network. Which protocol should they use?",["RDP","SSH","Telnet","SPICE"],1,"SSH provides an encrypted remote shell. Telnet sends everything, including passwords, in clear text.","Objective 4.9",["RDP gives a graphical remote desktop mainly for Windows; it's not the standard secure command-line tool for Linux.",null,"Telnet gives a remote shell but sends everything, including passwords, in clear text.","SPICE is a remote display protocol for virtual machines, not a secure command-line access protocol."],1],["ap89",0,4,"An employee pastes a customer contract into a free public AI chatbot to summarize it. What is the main concern?",["The summary will be too short","Chatbots cannot read contracts","Confidential data may be stored or used by the public model's provider","The contract will be deleted from the server"],2,"Public AI tools may retain and use submitted data, which risks privacy and confidentiality. Company policy should restrict this.","Objective 4.10",["Summary length is a quality issue, not the main concern; the risk is exposing confidential data.","Chatbots can read and summarize contracts; the concern is where the data goes, not capability.",null,"Pasting text into a chatbot doesn't delete the contract from the company's server."],3],["ap90",0,4,"An AI assistant gives a technician a command with a switch that does not exist. What AI limitation is this?",["Bias","Plagiarism","Data retention","Hallucination"],3,"A hallucination is confident but false output. AI answers must be checked for accuracy before use.","Objective 4.10",["Bias is systematic skew in AI output based on its training data; an invented command switch is a fabrication.","Plagiarism is copying others' work without credit; inventing a nonexistent switch is a different problem.","Data retention is about how long a provider keeps submitted data; it doesn't explain a made-up command.",null],1],["x1",0,1,"A Windows Server administrator is formatting a large storage volume that will hold virtual machine files. They want a Microsoft file system built for data integrity and resilience rather than for booting Windows. Which should they choose?",["FAT32","exFAT","ReFS","APFS"],2,"ReFS (Resilient File System) is Microsoft's file system designed for integrity checking and resilience on large data volumes such as VM storage. APFS is tempting because it is also modern, but it is Apple's file system and Windows does not use it natively.","Domain 1: File systems",["FAT32 is a legacy file system with a 4 GB file size limit and no integrity features.","exFAT is built for removable flash media, not resilient server data volumes.",null,"APFS is Apple's file system and is not natively supported by Windows Server."],2],["x2",0,1,"A Linux user formatted a USB drive as ext4 to match their Ubuntu laptop. When the drive is plugged into a Windows 11 PC, File Explorer offers to format it instead of showing the files. What is the most likely cause?",["Windows cannot read ext4 without extra software","Windows 11 does not support exFAT on USB drives","The drive has an NTFS volume that Linux locked","USB drives cannot use a GPT partition table"],0,"ext4 is a native Linux file system that Windows does not read out of the box, so Windows treats the volume as unformatted. Blaming exFAT is wrong because the drive is ext4, and Windows reads exFAT natively anyway.","Domain 1: File systems",[null,"Windows 11 reads and writes exFAT natively, and the drive is ext4 in any case.","The drive was formatted ext4, not NTFS, and Linux does not lock volumes that way.","USB drives can use GPT, and partition style would not make Windows offer to format a readable volume."],2],["x3",0,1,"A Mac user is erasing an external SSD in Disk Utility. It will only ever be used with Macs on a current macOS release and should support snapshots and native encryption. Which format fits best?",["exFAT","FAT32","NTFS","APFS"],3,"APFS is Apple's modern file system, optimized for SSDs and supporting snapshots and native encryption. exFAT is the tempting choice for cross-platform drives, but it lacks those features and is unnecessary when only Macs will use the disk.","Domain 1: File systems",["exFAT works across platforms but lacks APFS snapshots and native encryption.","FAT32 is a legacy format with a 4 GB file size limit and no snapshots or encryption.","macOS can only read NTFS by default, so it cannot write to an NTFS drive without extra software.",null],1],["x4",0,1,"A user's Windows 10 Pro PC meets the Windows 11 hardware requirements. They want to move to Windows 11 while keeping their installed apps, files and settings. Which installation type should the technician use?",["Clean install from a USB drive","In-place upgrade","Image deployment over PXE","Reset this PC from Settings"],1,"An in-place upgrade installs the new Windows version over the old one and keeps apps, files and settings. A clean install is tempting because it is very reliable, but it wipes the existing apps and settings.","Domain 1: Installations and upgrades",["A clean install erases the existing apps and settings the user wants to keep.",null,"Image deployment replaces the whole system with a standard image instead of keeping the user's setup.","Reset this PC reinstalls the same Windows version and removes apps; it does not upgrade to Windows 11."],1],["x5",0,1,"During a clean Windows install on a server with a hardware RAID controller, setup shows no disks to install to. The array is healthy in the controller's own configuration utility. What should the technician do?",["Convert the disks from MBR to GPT with diskpart","Create a new installation USB and restart setup","Load the RAID controller's storage driver during setup","Change the boot method from USB to PXE"],2,"Windows setup only sees disks it has a driver for; loading the vendor's third-party storage driver with the Load driver option exposes the array. Converting to GPT is tempting, but partition style does not matter when setup cannot see the disk at all.","Domain 1: Installations and upgrades",["Partition style does not matter when setup cannot detect the disk at all.","The install media works; the problem is that it has no driver for the RAID controller.",null,"PXE only changes where setup loads from; setup would still lack the storage driver."],3],["x6",0,1,"A Windows 11 PC has ongoing system corruption. sfc /scannow and DISM /RestoreHealth did not fix it, and the user wants to keep installed apps and files. What is the best next step?",["Run an in-place repair install of the same version","Do a clean install and restore files from backup","Reset the PC using the Remove everything option","Roll back the most recent driver in Device Manager"],0,"A repair install reinstalls the same Windows version over itself, replacing system files while keeping apps and data. A clean install would also fix corruption, but it removes the apps the user wants to keep.","Domain 1: Installations and upgrades",[null,"A clean install fixes corruption but removes all installed apps, which the user wants to keep.","Remove everything wipes apps and personal files, which is far more than the user wants.","Rolling back one driver will not repair system-wide corruption that sfc and DISM could not fix."],3],["x7",0,1,"A user with Windows 11 Home wants to connect to this home PC from work using the built-in Remote Desktop client. What is required on the home PC?",["Enable Remote Desktop in Settings > System","Allow TCP 3389 through Windows Firewall","Install the Remote Desktop app from the Store","Upgrade it to Windows 11 Pro or higher"],3,"Home editions can connect out with Remote Desktop but cannot act as a Remote Desktop host; Pro, Enterprise or Education is needed. Enabling it in Settings is tempting, but that option is not available on Home.","Domain 1: Windows editions",["The Remote Desktop host setting is not available on Windows 11 Home.","Opening the port does nothing because Home has no Remote Desktop host service to accept connections.","The client app lets a PC connect out; it does not let a Home PC accept incoming sessions.",null],2],["x8",0,1,"An internal website shows as untrusted. IT says the company's root CA certificate must be added to the Trusted Root Certification Authorities store for the user. Which tool should the technician open?",["lusrmgr.msc","certmgr.msc","gpedit.msc","devmgmt.msc"],1,"certmgr.msc is the Certificate Manager snap-in for the current user's certificate stores, including Trusted Root Certification Authorities. gpedit.msc can distribute certificates by policy, but it is not the tool for importing one on this PC directly.","Domain 1: Windows tools",["lusrmgr.msc manages local users and groups, not certificate stores.",null,"gpedit.msc edits local policy and is not the tool for importing a certificate into a store.","devmgmt.msc manages hardware devices and drivers, not certificates."],1],["x9",0,1,"A technician must record CPU, memory and disk queue counters on a server for 24 hours to prove a bottleneck and review the data afterward. Which tool should they use?",["Task Manager's Performance tab","Resource Monitor","Performance Monitor","Reliability Monitor"],2,"Performance Monitor can log chosen counters over time with a data collector set and replay them later. Resource Monitor is tempting because it shows detailed live usage, but it does not log counters over a long period.","Domain 1: Windows tools",["Task Manager shows live graphs only and cannot log chosen counters for 24 hours.","Resource Monitor shows detailed real-time usage but does not record counters over long periods.",null,"Reliability Monitor tracks crashes and changes over days, not performance counters."],3],["x10",0,1,"From inside Windows, a technician wants the PC to boot into Safe Mode with networking on every restart until they turn it off. Which tool sets this?",["System Configuration (msconfig)","Registry Editor (regedit)","Services (services.msc)","Disk Cleanup (cleanmgr)"],0,"The Boot tab of System Configuration has a Safe boot option with a Network choice that stays on until cleared. Registry Editor is tempting because it controls many settings, but it is not the supported tool for choosing a boot mode.","Domain 1: Windows tools",[null,"Editing the registry by hand is risky and is not the supported way to pick a boot mode.","services.msc starts and stops services; it has no boot mode setting.","Disk Cleanup removes temporary and unneeded files and has nothing to do with boot options."],2],["x11",0,1,"Files on a secondary NTFS data drive (D:) sometimes become unreadable, and Windows reports that the drive has file system errors. Which command should the technician run to check and fix them?",["format D: /q","diskpart, then clean","sfc /scannow","chkdsk D: /f"],3,"chkdsk with /f checks the volume's file system and fixes errors it finds. sfc /scannow is tempting because it repairs things, but it only repairs protected Windows system files, not a data volume's file system.","Domain 1: Command-line tools",["Formatting erases the volume, destroying the files instead of repairing the file system.","The diskpart clean command wipes the disk's partition information, which destroys the data.","sfc repairs protected Windows system files, not file system errors on a data drive.",null],2],["x12",0,1,"A user says a new Group Policy setting is not taking effect. The technician wants to see which Group Policy Objects were applied to the user and computer. Which command should they run?",["gpupdate /force","gpresult /r","whoami /groups","net user %username%"],1,"gpresult /r reports the Resultant Set of Policy, listing the GPOs applied to the computer and user. gpupdate /force is tempting, but it only reapplies policy and does not show which GPOs were applied.","Domain 1: Command-line tools",["gpupdate /force reapplies policy but does not report which GPOs were applied.",null,"whoami /groups shows the user's group memberships, not the applied policies.","net user shows account details such as password settings, not Group Policy results."],2],["x13",0,1,"A technician suspects a program on a Windows PC keeps a connection open to an unknown remote server. Which command lists active connections along with the owning process IDs?",["tracert","nslookup","netstat -ano","pathping"],2,"netstat -ano lists all connections and listening ports with numeric addresses and the PID that owns each one. pathping is tempting as a network tool, but it only measures the route and packet loss to one destination.","Domain 1: Command-line tools",["tracert shows the hops to a destination, not the connections open on this PC.","nslookup queries DNS records and does not list active connections.",null,"pathping measures route and packet loss to one host; it does not list local connections or PIDs."],1],["x14",0,1,"A Linux admin adds a data disk. It mounts correctly with the mount command but is missing after every reboot. Which file should be edited so it mounts automatically?",["/etc/fstab","/etc/hosts","/etc/resolv.conf","/etc/passwd"],0,"/etc/fstab lists file systems and their mount points to mount at boot. /etc/resolv.conf is tempting as another core config file, but it only sets DNS resolvers and has nothing to do with disks.","Domain 1: Linux",[null,"/etc/hosts maps hostnames to IP addresses and does not control mounting.","/etc/resolv.conf sets DNS resolvers and has nothing to do with disks.","/etc/passwd stores user account information, not mount points."],2],["x15",0,1,"On an Ubuntu workstation, a technician needs the process ID of a hung background process named reportd so they can stop it. Which command finds it?",["df -h | grep reportd","man reportd","find / -name reportd","ps aux | grep reportd"],3,"ps aux lists running processes with their PIDs, and grep filters for the name. find is tempting because it locates things, but it searches the file system for files, not running processes.","Domain 1: Linux",["df -h reports disk space usage by file system, not running processes.","man shows a manual page; it does not report whether the process is running or its PID.","find searches for files on disk, not processes that are running in memory.",null],2],["x16",0,1,"A Mac user downloaded a .dmg file that contains an application. What is the usual way to install the app?",["Mount the .dmg, then run its contents with sudo from Terminal","Open the .dmg and drag the app into the Applications folder","Rename the .dmg to .pkg and double-click it","Copy the .dmg file itself into the Applications folder"],1,"A .dmg is a disk image; opening it mounts it, and the .app inside is dragged to Applications to install it. Copying the .dmg itself is tempting, but that only stores the disk image and does not install the app.","Domain 1: macOS",["A .dmg normally holds an app to drag into Applications; running things with sudo is unnecessary and risky.",null,"Renaming the file does not change its format; a .dmg is not an installer package.","Copying the .dmg only stores the disk image; the app itself is never installed."],1],["x17",0,1,"A MacBook holding client files needs full-disk encryption so the data can't be read if the laptop is stolen. Which built-in feature should be turned on?",["Keychain Access","Time Machine","FileVault","Gatekeeper"],2,"FileVault encrypts the entire startup disk on a Mac. Keychain Access is tempting because it protects secrets, but it only stores passwords, keys and certificates, not the whole disk.","Domain 1: macOS",["Keychain Access stores passwords, keys and certificates, not full-disk encryption.","Time Machine makes backups; it does not encrypt the startup disk against theft.",null,"Gatekeeper checks that apps come from trusted developers; it does not encrypt data."],1],["x18",0,1,"A clinic still runs one PC on an operating system version that the vendor has declared end-of-life. What is the main risk of keeping it in use?",["It no longer gets security patches, so new flaws stay open","It will stop booting on the vendor's end-of-life date","Its installed software licenses become invalid","It can no longer join a workgroup or a domain"],0,"After end-of-life, the vendor stops releasing security updates, so newly found vulnerabilities remain unpatched. The idea that it stops booting is tempting but false: EOL systems keep running, which is exactly why the unpatched risk persists.","Domain 1: OS life cycles",[null,"End-of-life systems keep booting; the problem is that they stop getting fixes.","Existing licenses stay valid after end-of-life; security is the real concern.","An EOL system can still join a workgroup or domain; losing patches is the risk."],1],["x19",0,2,"On a shared Windows Pro PC, one user wants a single folder encrypted so that other people who sign in to the same PC cannot open it. Full-drive encryption is not required. Which feature fits?",["BitLocker drive encryption","BitLocker To Go","NTFS compression attribute","EFS"],3,"EFS (Encrypting File System) encrypts individual files and folders tied to a user's account, so other users on the same PC cannot open them. BitLocker is tempting, but once the volume is unlocked it does not stop other signed-in users who have NTFS access.","Domain 2: Windows security",["BitLocker protects the whole volume from offline access, but once unlocked other users with NTFS access can read files.","BitLocker To Go encrypts removable drives, not a folder on the internal disk.","Compression saves space; it does not encrypt or prevent other users from reading files.",null],3],["x20",0,2,"A company wants employees to sign in to their Windows laptops without typing a password, using the built-in fingerprint reader or IR face camera. Which feature should it enable?",["Windows Hello","Credential Manager","User Account Control","Microsoft Defender SmartScreen"],0,"Windows Hello provides biometric and PIN sign-in on Windows. Credential Manager is tempting because it relates to sign-in, but it only stores saved credentials for sites and network resources.","Domain 2: Windows security",[null,"Credential Manager stores saved credentials for sites and resources; it does not do biometric sign-in.","UAC prompts for elevation; it does not provide passwordless sign-in.","SmartScreen warns about unsafe sites and downloads; it has no sign-in function."],1],["x21",0,2,"An admin grants the HR group Modify on the D:\\HR folder. A new subfolder created inside shows the same entry for HR, but its checkboxes are greyed out. Why?",["The subfolder's creator locked the permissions","The permissions are inherited from the parent folder","Share permissions override NTFS on subfolders","UAC blocks editing permissions on new folders"],1,"Greyed-out permission entries are inherited from the parent; change them on the parent or disable inheritance on the subfolder. Blaming share permissions is tempting, but share permissions never appear in the NTFS Security tab.","Domain 2: Windows security",["Creators can own a folder, but ownership does not grey out entries; inheritance does.",null,"Share permissions never appear in the NTFS Security tab, so they cannot grey out these entries.","UAC may ask for elevation, but it does not grey out individual permission entries."],3],["x22",0,2,"An older SOHO access point is set to WPA2 with TKIP. All client devices support AES. What change improves wireless security without new hardware?",["Switch security to WEP for older clients","Hide the SSID from broadcasts","Change the encryption to AES (CCMP)","Enable WPS for easier joining"],2,"TKIP is a legacy, weaker cipher; WPA2 with AES (CCMP) is the secure setting and every client supports it. Hiding the SSID is tempting, but the SSID is still visible in client traffic and it does nothing about the weak cipher.","Domain 2: Wireless security",["WEP is broken and much weaker than even WPA2 with TKIP.","Hiding the SSID is not real security, since the name still appears in client traffic.",null,"WPS has known PIN weaknesses and should be disabled, not enabled."],2],["x23",0,2,"Why is WPA3-Personal more resistant than WPA2-Personal to an attacker who captures the handshake and tries to guess the passphrase offline?",["It authenticates each user through a RADIUS server","It encrypts every frame with TKIP instead of AES","It hides the SSID so attackers cannot capture it","It uses SAE in place of the WPA2 PSK handshake"],3,"WPA3-Personal uses Simultaneous Authentication of Equals (SAE), which prevents offline dictionary attacks against a captured handshake. RADIUS is tempting, but that is Enterprise mode, not Personal, which still uses a shared passphrase.","Domain 2: Wireless security",["RADIUS is used by WPA3-Enterprise, not WPA3-Personal, which still uses a passphrase.","TKIP is a legacy cipher that WPA3 does not use at all.","Hiding the SSID does not stop handshake capture and is not part of WPA3.",null],3],["x24",0,2,"An employee's bank and email accounts were accessed even though they use strong, unique passwords. A scan finds a hidden program recording every keystroke and sending the data out. What type of malware is this?",["Keylogger","Adware","Ransomware","Boot sector virus"],0,"A keylogger captures keystrokes, including passwords, and sends them to the attacker. Adware is tempting because it is also unwanted software, but it shows ads rather than stealing typed credentials.","Domain 2: Malware",[null,"Adware shows unwanted ads; it does not capture typed passwords.","Ransomware encrypts files and demands payment; it does not quietly log keystrokes.","A boot sector virus infects startup code; it is not designed to record keystrokes."],1],["x25",0,2,"EDR alerts that Word launched PowerShell, which ran encoded commands entirely in memory. The antivirus found no malicious file on disk. What type of threat is most likely?",["Boot sector virus","Fileless malware","Adware","Rootkit"],1,"Fileless malware lives in memory and abuses built-in tools like PowerShell, leaving little or nothing on disk for signature scans. Rootkit is tempting because both are stealthy, but a rootkit hides itself by modifying the OS rather than running from a macro-launched script in memory.","Domain 2: Malware",["A boot sector virus lives in startup code on disk, which does not match memory-only PowerShell activity.",null,"Adware shows unwanted ads and does not usually run encoded PowerShell from a macro.","A rootkit hides itself by modifying the OS, not by running script commands in memory from a document."],3],["x26",0,2,"A company wants to spot suspicious behavior on laptops, record process activity for investigations, and isolate an infected laptop from the network from a central console. Which tool fits?",["Host-based firewall","Email security gateway","EDR","Signature-only antivirus"],2,"Endpoint detection and response (EDR) monitors endpoint behavior, records activity for investigation and can isolate hosts remotely. Signature-only antivirus is tempting, but it matches known files and does not provide behavioral recording or remote isolation.","Domain 2: Malware tools",["A host firewall filters traffic but does not record process activity for investigations.","An email gateway filters mail before it reaches users; it does not monitor endpoints.",null,"Signature-based antivirus matches known files and lacks behavior recording and remote isolation."],2],["x27",0,2,"A user suspects someone secretly installed an app on her phone that reports her location, messages and calls to another person. Which category of malicious software is this?",["Adware","Cryptominer","Ransomware","Stalkerware"],3,"Stalkerware is covert monitoring software that sends a victim's location and communications to another person. A cryptominer is tempting because it also runs hidden, but it uses the device to mine currency and does not track the user.","Domain 2: Malware",["Adware displays ads; it does not secretly report location and messages to a person.","A cryptominer uses the device's CPU to mine currency and does not track the user.","Ransomware locks data for payment and makes itself known, unlike covert monitoring.",null],1],["x28",0,2,"The CFO receives an email that mentions a real pending acquisition by name and urges an urgent, confidential wire transfer to close the deal. What type of attack is this?",["Whaling","Vishing","Smishing","Tailgating"],0,"Whaling is phishing aimed at senior executives, using details that make the message believable. Vishing is tempting as another phishing variant, but vishing is done by voice call, not email.","Domain 2: Social engineering",[null,"Vishing uses voice calls, but this attack arrived by email.","Smishing uses text messages, not email.","Tailgating is a physical entry technique, not a message-based attack."],2],["x29",0,2,"A person carrying a stack of boxes follows an employee through a badge-controlled door without badging in. What is this called?",["Shoulder surfing","Tailgating","Dumpster diving","Evil twin"],1,"Tailgating is following an authorized person through a secured entrance without authenticating. Shoulder surfing is tempting as another in-person technique, but it means watching someone's screen or keypad to steal information.","Domain 2: Social engineering",["Shoulder surfing means watching someone's screen or keypad, not entering behind them.",null,"Dumpster diving means searching trash for information, not entering a building.","An evil twin is a fake Wi-Fi access point, not a physical entry technique."],1],["x30",0,2,"A company wants to donate working hard disk drives to a school. Data must not be recoverable, but the drives must stay usable. What should the technician do?",["Run a standard quick format in Windows","Degauss each drive with a bulk eraser","Overwrite all sectors with a disk-wiping tool","Delete the partitions with diskpart"],2,"Overwriting every sector with a wiping tool removes the data while leaving the drive reusable. Degaussing is tempting because it erases magnetic data, but it also destroys the servo information and leaves a hard drive unusable.","Domain 2: Data destruction",["A quick format leaves most data in place, so it can be recovered with common tools.","Degaussing erases magnetic data but also makes a hard drive unusable.",null,"Deleting partitions leaves the data on the platters, where recovery tools can find it."],3],["x31",0,2,"An attacker posts a comment on a company web forum that contains a script. The script then runs in the browser of every visitor who views the comment. What attack is this?",["SQL injection","On-path attack","Zero-day exploit","XSS"],3,"Cross-site scripting (XSS) injects script into a site so it runs in other users' browsers. SQL injection is tempting because both abuse input fields, but SQL injection targets the site's database queries, not visitors' browsers.","Domain 2: Threats",["SQL injection targets the site's database queries, not scripts in visitors' browsers.","An on-path attack intercepts traffic between two parties; it does not store scripts on a site.","A zero-day is an unpatched unknown flaw, not the name of this script-injection technique.",null],2],["x32",0,2,"A technician has verified malware symptoms on a Windows PC and quarantined it from the network. According to CompTIA's malware removal procedure, what is the next step?",["Disable System Restore in Windows","Update anti-malware software and scan","Schedule scans and run OS updates","Educate the end user"],0,"The order is: investigate and verify symptoms, quarantine, disable System Restore, remediate (update and scan), schedule scans and updates, re-enable System Restore and create a restore point, and educate the user. Updating and scanning is tempting, but it comes after System Restore is disabled.","Domain 2: Malware removal",[null,"Updating and scanning is the remediation step, which comes after System Restore is disabled.","Scheduling scans and updates comes after the malware has been removed.","Educating the user is the final step of the procedure, not the third."],2],["x33",0,2,"An audit finds that employees often walk away from unlocked PCs in open areas. Which hardening step directly addresses this?",["Disable the built-in Guest account on every PC","Set a timed screen lock that requires sign-in","Enforce password expiration every 90 days","Turn off AutoPlay for all removable media"],1,"A screen saver or inactivity lock that requires sign-in protects PCs left unattended. Disabling the Guest account is tempting as a hardening step, but it does nothing to protect a session that is already signed in and left open.","Domain 2: Workstation hardening",["Disabling Guest is good hardening but does not protect a session that is already open.",null,"Password expiration changes how often passwords change, not what happens at an open, unattended PC.","Disabling AutoPlay stops USB auto-launch; it does not lock an unattended screen."],2],["x34",0,2,"During a home router review, a technician sees that a device on the LAN opened inbound ports on the router without anyone configuring them. Which setting should be disabled?",["WPS","The guest network","UPnP","DHCP reservations"],2,"UPnP lets LAN devices ask the router to open ports automatically, which malware can abuse. WPS is tempting because it is also commonly disabled, but WPS is for joining Wi-Fi with a PIN or button and does not open ports.","Domain 2: SOHO router security",["WPS is used to join Wi-Fi with a PIN or button; it does not open inbound ports.","The guest network isolates visitors; it does not let LAN devices open router ports.",null,"DHCP reservations fix a device's IP address; they do not open ports on the router."],2],["x35",0,2,"A small office wants visitors to get internet access over Wi-Fi without being able to reach office PCs, printers or file shares. What should the technician configure?",["Share the main Wi-Fi passphrase with guests","Turn on MAC filtering for the main SSID","Create DHCP reservations for visitors","Enable the router's isolated guest network"],3,"A guest network gives visitors internet access while separating them from the internal LAN. MAC filtering is tempting, but it only controls which devices can join and would still put visitors on the same network as office systems.","Domain 2: SOHO router security",["Sharing the main passphrase puts visitors on the same network as office systems.","MAC filtering only controls who can join; visitors would still reach office devices.","DHCP reservations assign fixed addresses; they do not separate visitors from the LAN.",null],2],["x36",0,2,"A user believes private (incognito) browsing hides everything they do from the company network. What does private browsing actually do?",["It stops saving local history and cookies after the window closes","It encrypts traffic so the office network can't see which sites load","It hides the device's IP address from every website","It blocks all malware downloads during the session"],0,"Private browsing only avoids keeping local history, cookies and form data after the session; the network, proxy and sites can still see the traffic. Encrypting traffic from the network is tempting, but that describes a VPN, not private browsing.","Domain 2: Browser security",[null,"Private browsing does not encrypt traffic; that is the role of HTTPS or a VPN.","Websites still see the device's public IP address in private browsing.","Private browsing does not include malware scanning or block downloads."],2],["x37",0,3,"Windows shows a low memory warning on a PC with plenty of RAM. Task Manager shows one line-of-business app's memory use climbing steadily for hours until the warning appears, even with nothing else open. What is the best fix?",["Enlarge the paging file and consider the issue resolved","Update or patch the app, which likely leaks memory","Replace the system drive with a faster SSD","Run chkdsk /f on the system drive"],1,"Memory that grows steadily in one process until it runs out is the classic sign of a memory leak, so the app needs a fix or update. A larger paging file is tempting, but it only delays the warning while the leak keeps growing.","Domain 3: Windows troubleshooting",["A larger paging file only delays the warning while the leaking app keeps using more memory.",null,"A faster drive does not stop one app from using more memory over time.","chkdsk repairs file system errors and has nothing to do with memory consumption."],3],["x38",0,3,"After a user installed a free system utility, several apps stopped working, and uninstalling the utility did not help. A restore point exists from before the install, and the user's documents must be kept. What should the technician do?",["Reset this PC and choose the Remove everything option","Reimage the PC from the corporate image","Use System Restore to the point before the install","Run DISM to repair the component store"],2,"System Restore rolls back system files, drivers, registry settings and installed programs to the restore point without touching personal documents. Reimaging is tempting because it is thorough, but it wipes the PC when a less drastic fix is available.","Domain 3: Windows fixes",["Removing everything wipes the user's documents, which must be kept.","Reimaging wipes the PC when a less drastic rollback is available.",null,"DISM repairs the Windows component store; it does not undo changes a third-party utility made."],2],["x39",0,3,"A Windows PC loops on Automatic Repair and never reaches the desktop, and Safe Mode will not load either. Where can the technician run Startup Repair, System Restore or a Command Prompt?",["Task Manager's Startup apps tab","Event Viewer's System log","System Configuration (msconfig)","Windows Recovery Environment"],3,"The Windows Recovery Environment (WinRE) runs outside the installed OS and offers Startup Repair, System Restore, uninstall updates and Command Prompt. msconfig is tempting because it controls boot options, but it only runs from inside a working Windows session.","Domain 3: Safe Mode and WinRE",["Task Manager runs inside Windows, which cannot reach the desktop here.","Event Viewer also needs a working Windows session and does not offer repair tools.","msconfig only runs from inside a working Windows session.",null],2],["x40",0,3,"A shared kiosk PC has had repeated infections and crashes. sfc, DISM and driver rollbacks have been tried over several weeks. The company has a standard image, and no user data is stored locally. What is the most efficient fix?",["Reimage it from the standard corporate image","Run another round of sfc and DISM repairs","Roll back each recently updated driver again","Create a new local profile for the kiosk user"],0,"When repairs keep failing and there is no local data to lose, reimaging returns the PC to a known-good state fastest. A new profile is tempting, but it would not remove system-level corruption or persistent malware.","Domain 3: Windows fixes",[null,"sfc and DISM have already failed repeatedly, so another round is unlikely to help.","Driver rollbacks were already tried and do not address repeated infections.","A new profile does not remove system-level corruption or malware."],3],["x41",0,3,"A user's phone will not complete tap-to-pay at store terminals, although other phones work at the same terminals. Wi-Fi and Bluetooth work normally. What should the technician check first?",["Toggle Bluetooth off and on, then try again","Confirm NFC is on and a wallet app is default","Forget and rejoin the store's Wi-Fi network","Factory reset the phone to clear settings"],1,"Tap-to-pay uses NFC, so NFC must be enabled and a payment app set as the default. Toggling Bluetooth is tempting because it is also short-range wireless, but contactless payments do not use Bluetooth.","Domain 3: Mobile OS issues",["Contactless payment uses NFC, not Bluetooth.",null,"Tap-to-pay does not depend on the store's Wi-Fi network.","A factory reset is drastic and should come after simple checks like whether NFC is on."],2],["x42",0,3,"An app on a user's phone will not update from the official app store, and the error says there is not enough space. What should the technician do?",["Factory reset the phone and restore a backup","Sign out of every account on the phone","Free storage space, then retry the update","Enable developer options and sideload it"],2,"The error points directly at storage, so removing unused apps, media or cache and retrying is the fix. A factory reset is tempting as a cure-all, but it is drastic and unnecessary for a simple storage shortage.","Domain 3: Mobile OS issues",["A factory reset is drastic and unnecessary when the error simply says storage is full.","Signing out of accounts does not free storage space for the update.",null,"Sideloading bypasses store protections and still needs free storage."],2],["x43",0,3,"A corporate phone keeps triggering data-limit alerts, though the user barely uses it. Settings show a recently installed flashlight app using large amounts of background data. What should the technician do?",["Upgrade to a larger cellular data plan","Turn off Wi-Fi calling in settings","Clear the cache of every installed app","Remove the flashlight app and scan the phone"],3,"Unexpected high network traffic from a simple app is a sign of malicious or spyware behavior, so remove it and scan. A larger data plan is tempting because it stops the alerts, but it ignores the likely data theft.","Domain 3: Mobile security issues",["A bigger plan hides the symptom while a likely malicious app keeps sending data.","Wi-Fi calling has nothing to do with a flashlight app's background data.","Clearing cache does not stop the app from sending data in the background.",null],2],["x44",0,3,"On one PC only, typing the bank's address in any browser opens a look-alike site. The DNS server settings are correct, and other PCs on the same network are fine. What should the technician check?",["Inspect the hosts file for tampered entries","Clear the cache in the main browser","Reinstall the user's default browser","Update the network adapter driver"],0,"Because every browser on this one PC is affected while DNS settings are correct, a system-level redirect such as a modified hosts file is the likely cause. Clearing one browser's cache is tempting, but it would not explain the redirect happening in every browser.","Domain 3: Browser security issues",[null,"Clearing one browser's cache would not explain every browser on the PC being redirected.","The problem appears in all browsers, so reinstalling one will not fix it.","A driver problem would cause no connectivity, not a redirect to a look-alike site."],3],["x45",0,3,"A user's browser has become very slow. CPU use is high only while the browser is open, and it has many extensions installed. What should the technician try first?",["Flush the DNS cache with ipconfig /flushdns","Disable all extensions, then test again","Reinstall Windows with a clean install","Ask the ISP to upgrade the connection"],1,"Extensions run inside the browser and are a common cause of high CPU and slowness, so disabling them isolates the cause. Flushing DNS is tempting, but DNS problems cause failed lookups rather than high CPU.","Domain 3: Browser security issues",["Flushing DNS fixes name lookups; it does not reduce high CPU use.",null,"Reinstalling Windows is drastic before trying simple, targeted checks like extensions.","A faster connection does not reduce CPU load inside the browser."],2],["x46",0,3,"After malware was removed, a scam pop-up still appears shortly after every reboot, although full scans now come back clean. Where should the technician look for the leftover launcher?",["Disk Management volumes","Device Manager's driver list","Task Scheduler and startup apps","Certificate Manager's user store"],2,"Malware often leaves a scheduled task or startup entry that relaunches a pop-up at each sign-in. Certificate Manager is tempting because malware can add rogue certificates, but a certificate does not launch a program at startup.","Domain 3: Post-infection cleanup",["Disk Management manages partitions and volumes, not programs that launch at startup.","Device Manager lists hardware drivers, not the tasks or apps that start at sign-in.",null,"A certificate cannot launch a program; startup tasks and entries do."],3],["x47",0,3,"Windows keeps showing notifications that claim viruses were found and urge the user to click. They come from a website the user once allowed to send notifications. What is the best fix?",["Reinstall the antivirus product","Pause Windows Update for a week","Turn off all Windows Security notifications in Settings","Revoke the site's browser notification permission"],3,"The alerts come from a site granted notification permission, so revoking that permission stops them. Turning off Windows Security notifications is tempting, but it would hide real alerts and leave the fake ones running.","Domain 3: PC security issues",["Reinstalling antivirus will not revoke a website's permission to send notifications.","Pausing Windows Update has nothing to do with website notifications.","This hides genuine security alerts while the fake site notifications continue.",null],2],["x48",0,3,"A PC blue-screens at random times with different stop codes each time, and Windows Memory Diagnostic reports errors. What should the technician do?",["Replace the RAM module that failed testing","Reinstall the graphics driver from the vendor","Run Disk Cleanup to free system space","Turn off fast startup in Power Options"],0,"Random BSODs with varying stop codes plus memory test errors point to faulty RAM. A graphics driver reinstall is tempting because drivers often cause BSODs, but a driver fault usually gives a consistent stop code and would not fail a memory test.","Domain 3: Windows troubleshooting",[null,"A driver fault usually gives a consistent stop code and would not fail a memory test.","Low disk space does not cause random BSODs with memory test errors.","Fast startup affects shutdown and boot, not memory errors shown by diagnostics."],2],["x49",0,4,"A contract says the managed service provider must respond to critical tickets within one hour and restore service within four hours. Which document is this?",["Acceptable use policy","Service-level agreement","Standard operating procedure","Knowledge base article"],1,"A service-level agreement (SLA) defines measurable service commitments such as response and resolution times. An SOP is tempting because it also guides support work, but it describes how to perform a task, not the provider's contractual commitments.","Domain 4: Documentation",["An AUP tells users what they may and may not do with company systems.",null,"An SOP gives step-by-step instructions for tasks, not contractual response times.","A knowledge base article documents how to fix issues, not service commitments."],1],["x50",0,4,"A tier 1 technician followed the knowledge base steps but cannot fix a file server outage that affects the whole sales department. What should they do?",["Close the ticket and tell users to reboot","Keep working alone without updating the ticket","Raise the severity and escalate with clear notes","Open a separate ticket for each affected user"],2,"A department-wide outage is high severity, and when first-line steps fail it should be escalated with notes so the next tier does not repeat work. Opening a ticket per user is tempting, but it splits one incident and hides its real impact.","Domain 4: Ticketing",["Closing an unresolved outage ticket hides a major problem and leaves users without service.","Working silently wastes time on a high-impact outage and leaves no record for others.",null,"Separate tickets split one incident and hide its real scope."],2],["x51",0,4,"A server gets a full backup on Sunday and incremental backups every other night. It fails on Thursday morning. Which backups are needed for the restore?",["Only the most recent incremental backup","Sunday's full plus Wednesday's incremental","Sunday's full backup by itself","Sunday's full plus Mon–Wed incrementals"],3,"Each incremental holds only changes since the previous backup, so the full plus every incremental since it must be restored in order. Full plus Wednesday only is tempting because that is how differentials work, but it would miss Monday's and Tuesday's changes.","Domain 4: Backup and recovery",["The latest incremental only holds Wednesday's changes, not the full data set.","Skipping Monday's and Tuesday's incrementals would lose those days' changes.","The full alone would lose everything changed Monday through Wednesday.",null],3],["x52",0,4,"A branch server has a slow WAN link. The company wants a weekly full backup without copying the whole data set across the link, so the backup server builds the full from the last full and the incrementals. What is this called?",["Synthetic full backup","Differential backup","Incremental backup","Grandfather-father-son rotation"],0,"A synthetic full is assembled on the backup side from an earlier full plus later incrementals, without reading all data from the source again. Differential is tempting, but it is just the changes since the last full, not a new full backup.","Domain 4: Backup and recovery",[null,"A differential holds changes since the last full; it is not a new full backup.","An incremental holds changes since the last backup, not a complete full.","GFS is a media rotation scheme, not a way of building a full backup."],3],["x53",0,4,"Before rolling a new version of the accounting app out to everyone, the change advisory board wants proof it works with company data and add-ons without risking production. What should the requester do?",["Deploy it to all users on a Friday evening","Test it in a sandbox that mirrors production","Write the rollback plan after deployment","Skip testing because the vendor tested it"],1,"Sandbox testing in a copy of production shows how the change behaves without putting live systems at risk. Relying on vendor testing is tempting, but the vendor has not tested it with this company's data and add-ons.","Domain 4: Change management",["Deploying to everyone without testing risks a company-wide outage.",null,"The rollback plan must be written before the change, not after.","The vendor has not tested with this company's data and add-ons."],2],["x54",0,4,"A small electrical fire starts inside a UPS in the server room. Which extinguisher is appropriate?",["A pressurized-water extinguisher","A Class A foam extinguisher","A CO2 (Class C) extinguisher","A wet towel thrown over the UPS"],2,"Electrical fires need a non-conductive agent such as CO2, which is rated for Class C electrical fires in the US system. Foam is tempting because it smothers fires, but water-based foam conducts electricity and is not rated for live electrical equipment.","Domain 4: Safety",["Water conducts electricity and is unsafe on live electrical equipment.","Water-based foam is for ordinary combustibles and can conduct electricity.",null,"A wet towel adds conductive water and does not reliably put out an electrical fire."],1],["x55",0,4,"A technician collects several swollen lithium-ion laptop batteries during a refresh. How should they be disposed of?",["Put them in regular trash inside a sealed bag","Puncture them first to release the pressure","Keep them in a warm supply closet until pickup","Recycle them per local hazardous-waste rules"],3,"Lithium-ion batteries must go to an approved recycler or hazardous-waste program according to local regulations. Sealing them in regular trash is tempting because it seems contained, but they can ignite and are generally barred from normal waste.","Domain 4: Environment",["Lithium-ion batteries can ignite and are generally not allowed in regular trash.","Puncturing a lithium-ion battery can cause fire or explosion.","Heat increases the risk of a damaged battery catching fire.",null],1],["x56",0,4,"A laptop that may hold evidence of fraud passes from the technician to the security team and then to law enforcement. What documents every hand-off of the device?",["Chain of custody","Change request form","Asset tag record","Certificate of destruction"],0,"Chain of custody records who had the evidence, when and why, so it stays admissible. The asset tag record is tempting because it tracks devices, but it records ownership and assignment, not evidence handling.","Domain 4: Incident response",[null,"A change request covers planned IT changes, not evidence handling.","An asset tag record tracks ownership and assignment, not who handled evidence.","A certificate of destruction proves disposal, which is the opposite of preserving evidence."],1],["x57",0,4,"A US-based online shop starts selling to customers in France and Germany and stores their names and home addresses. Which regulation most directly governs this personal data?",["PCI DSS","GDPR","HIPAA","DRM"],1,"The GDPR protects personal data of people in the EU, regardless of where the company is based. PCI DSS is tempting because the shop takes payments, but it covers cardholder data, not names and addresses in general.","Domain 4: Privacy and regulations",["PCI DSS covers payment card data, not names and addresses in general.",null,"HIPAA covers US health information, not retail customer data.","DRM is copy protection for digital content, not a privacy regulation."],2],["x58",0,4,"On a support call, a technician tells a non-technical user that 'DNS resolution is failing due to a stale resolver cache.' The user sounds confused. What should the technician do?",["Repeat the same explanation more slowly","Tell the user the details are too technical to go into","Explain in plain terms and confirm they understand","Transfer the call to a senior technician"],2,"Good communication means avoiding jargon, using plain language and checking understanding. Repeating it slowly is tempting, but the problem is the jargon, not the speed.","Domain 4: Professionalism",["Speaking slowly does not help when the jargon itself is the problem.","Refusing to explain is unprofessional and leaves the user uninformed.",null,"The technician can resolve this by communicating better; escalation is unnecessary."],2],["x59",0,4,"A Linux server with only its default tools needs a script that compresses old log files, run nightly by cron. Which script type fits best?",["A .bat batch file","A .vbs VBScript file","A .ps1 PowerShell script","A .sh shell script"],3,"Shell scripts (.sh) run natively on Linux and are the standard way to automate tasks with cron. PowerShell is tempting because it can run on Linux, but it is not installed by default, while a shell is.","Domain 4: Scripting",["Batch files run on Windows, not on a Linux server.","VBScript is a Windows scripting language that does not run natively on Linux.","PowerShell can run on Linux but is not installed by default.",null],1],["x60",0,4,"Staff need to reach their office Windows PCs from home using Remote Desktop. Which approach is most secure?",["Require a VPN with MFA and keep RDP off the internet","Port-forward TCP 3389 on the router to each office PC","Move RDP to a nonstandard port and forward that","Allow RDP only during normal business hours"],0,"Keeping RDP behind a VPN protected by MFA stops attackers from reaching it directly from the internet. A nonstandard port is tempting, but scanners find it quickly and the service is still exposed to password attacks.","Domain 4: Remote access",[null,"Forwarding 3389 exposes RDP directly to internet password and exploit attacks.","A nonstandard port is quickly found by scanners and RDP stays exposed.","Limiting hours still leaves RDP exposed to the internet during those hours."],3]]);
